WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.108
This is Second Story Studio, a fictional creative reuse studio in Beirut. Tala is its virtual
concierge.

2
00:00:09.108 --> 00:00:16.073
This demonstration follows the assignment requirements, showing the business files, identity,
router, real

3
00:00:16.073 --> 00:00:22.502
model-selected tools, conversation memory, reflection, and the deployed application. All customer
details

4
00:00:22.502 --> 00:00:24.645
used here are fictional.

5
00:00:25.917 --> 00:00:33.239
The repository includes the actual business summary and a two-page business profile PDF, alongside
the main

6
00:00:33.239 --> 00:00:40.104
notebook, application, and dependency list. Both business sources are loaded into the system
prompt. The

7
00:00:40.104 --> 00:00:47.885
profile defines the mission, services, team, and value proposition. The real API key remains in a
private

8
00:00:47.885 --> 00:00:51.546
environment file; only an empty example is submitted.

9
00:00:52.750 --> 00:01:00.215
Persona describes who Tala is: a warm, practical virtual concierge. Instructions separately define
how it

10
00:01:00.215 --> 00:01:08.177
must behave. These include grounding facts in the supplied knowledge, asking only for missing lead
details,

11
00:01:08.177 --> 00:01:15.145
logging unanswered business questions, and reporting tool actions truthfully. The notebook labels
these as

12
00:01:15.145 --> 00:01:17.135
two distinct prompt components.

13
00:01:18.417 --> 00:01:25.190
The classifier selects general questions, lead collection, or feedback and unanswered-question
handling. A

14
00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:33.006
dispatcher invokes the matching handler. The model then chooses appropriate tools, and a separate
tool

15
00:01:33.006 --> 00:01:38.738
dispatcher validates their arguments. The required customer-interest and unanswered-question tools
are

16
00:01:38.738 --> 00:01:44.990
implemented. A third tool handles customer suggestions without overloading the unanswered-question
schema.

17
00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:52.960
This is the actual Gradio ChatInterface, connected to OpenAI. A consultation question receives the
published

18
00:01:52.960 --> 00:02:00.118
price of twenty-five US dollars and duration of twenty minutes. The audit view shows the actual

19
00:02:00.118 --> 00:02:06.828
general-question route and no logging tool. Ordinary business questions do not require the
customer to

20
00:02:06.828 --> 00:02:08.170
provide contact details.

21
00:02:09.417 --> 00:02:17.053
The customer requests team follow-up about a chair, keeping the carved back. Tala asks for the
missing name

22
00:02:17.053 --> 00:02:23.841
and email. Those details arrive in a separate message. The agent retains the earlier project
description,

23
00:02:23.841 --> 00:02:30.629
calls the customer-interest tool, and confirms the saved record only after a successful tool
receipt. The

24
00:02:30.629 --> 00:02:34.871
request does not confirm an appointment or send an email.

25
00:02:36.083 --> 00:02:43.113
This mixed message asks about an unpublished warranty and suggests Sunday workshops. The model
records the

26
00:02:43.113 --> 00:02:48.385
unresolved warranty through record feedback, and the workshop suggestion through the separate

27
00:02:48.385 --> 00:02:54.976
customer-feedback tool. The evidence view shows actual JSON records and success receipts. The lead
record

28
00:02:54.976 --> 00:03:00.688
contains the fictional contact details and the chair description from the earlier turn.

29
00:03:01.917 --> 00:03:08.597
Reflection runs before the final reply is displayed. To make correction visible, this controlled
test

30
00:03:08.597 --> 00:03:15.278
deliberately supplies an incorrect price, duration, and booking promise. The second OpenAI call
checks the

31
00:03:15.278 --> 00:03:21.513
trusted business instructions and produces a corrected customer-facing reply. Both original and
revised text

32
00:03:21.513 --> 00:03:29.530
are logged. This flawed draft is explicitly a test input, not a claim that the normal model
spontaneously

33
00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:30.866
made these mistakes.

34
00:03:32.083 --> 00:03:38.722
Starting a new conversation clears the visible chat and its private session state. When asked
whether it

35
00:03:38.722 --> 00:03:45.361
knows the earlier customer name, Tala says that no name has been provided in the new conversation.

36
00:03:45.361 --> 00:03:51.219
Previously saved business records remain in the logs; clearing a conversation does not delete
them.

37
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.309
Here is the real public Hugging Face deployment. It serves the same Gradio assistant and answers a
business

38
00:04:00.309 --> 00:04:07.250
question using the server-side OpenAI key. The Space is running on ZeroGPU hosting, while model
inference

39
00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:14.192
remains on OpenAI. An unused startup compatibility function is never called by customer requests,
so the

40
00:04:14.192 --> 00:04:16.795
chat does not request GPU work.

41
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:25.846
The recorded test output shows fifty-two passing offline checks. They cover routing, validation,
tool

42
00:04:25.846 --> 00:04:33.693
protocols, session isolation, retries, and reflection failures. The notebook also runs its non-
network cells

43
00:04:33.693 --> 00:04:40.978
successfully. The GitHub submission contains the source, required business artifacts, video, and
synthetic

44
00:04:40.978 --> 00:04:48.264
evidence. Private credentials and personal customer logs are excluded. This completes the
demonstrated

45
00:04:48.264 --> 00:04:52.748
checklist, including reflection and a running hosted application.
